<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
		ENTITLEMENT PLACEHOLDERS — NOT ACTIVE.

		This file is deliberately NOT referenced by CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS in
		Config/App-iOS.xcconfig. Entitlements require a real Apple Developer
		team and matching provisioning, so wiring them in by default would make
		a fresh clone fail to build for anyone who has not configured signing.

		To turn them on:
		  1. Set SUNNIE_DEVELOPMENT_TEAM in Config/Shared.xcconfig.
		  2. Replace the container identifier below with your own.
		  3. Uncomment CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS in Config/App-iOS.xcconfig.
		  4. Pass `cloudKit: true` to ModelContainerFactory.make.

		Until then the app runs local-only, which is the documented default
		behaviour anyway: writes go to local storage first and synchronise
		later (TECHNICAL_ARCHITECTURE.md §12).
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.icloud-container-identifiers</key>
	<array>
		<string>iCloud.com.sunniedays.app</string>
	</array>
	<key>com.apple.developer.icloud-services</key>
	<array>
		<string>CloudKit</string>
	</array>
	<key>com.apple.developer.ubiquity-kvstore-identifier</key>
	<string>$(TeamIdentifierPrefix)$(CFBundleIdentifier)</string>

	<!--
		WeatherKit, for trip weather (TRAVEL_AND_FLIGHT_ATTENDANT.md §9).

		Inactive like the rest of this file. Weather is entirely optional: with
		no entitlement `SunnieWeatherService` returns nil and the trip screen
		simply leaves the weather line out, which is the same path it takes when
		offline. Nothing about a trip depends on it.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.weatherkit</key>
	<false/>

	<!--
		HealthKit (HEALTH_WATCH_WIDGETS_AND_INTENTS.md §2, §3).

		Inactive like the rest of this file. With no entitlement,
		`HKHealthStore.isHealthDataAvailable()` is the guard every path already
		checks: reads return absent values, writes return nil, and the Health
		section of Settings says the device has no Health store. Nothing about
		the app depends on it (§1).
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.healthkit</key>
	<false/>
	<key>com.apple.developer.healthkit-access</key>
	<array/>

	<!--
		Default data protection for the whole container.

		This is the mechanism that covers the SwiftData store, which cannot be given
		a protection class through `ModelConfiguration` the way an individual file
		can. With it, every file the app writes — the store, its journal and
		write-ahead files included — is unreadable until the device has been unlocked
		once since boot.

		`UntilFirstUserAuthentication` rather than `Complete`: the stronger class
		makes files unreadable whenever the screen is locked, which would break
		launch housekeeping, notification handling, and background Watch delivery.
		This class defends the realistic threat — a device taken while powered off —
		without breaking anything that runs unattended.

		Inactive like the rest of this file (ADR-012). The media store sets the same
		class per file in `SwiftDataMediaRepository`, which does take effect today
		and does not depend on signing.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.default-data-protection</key>
	<string>NSFileProtectionCompleteUntilFirstUserAuthentication</string>

	<!--
		App Group, shared with the widget extension.

		This is what lets the extension read the snapshot the app writes. The
		identifier must match `WidgetSnapshotStore.appGroupIdentifier` and the
		extension's own entitlements file exactly. Without it the app's write is
		a no-op and the widgets show their "open Sunnie Days" state.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>group.com.sunniedays.app</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
